    show: `Whether the same knots are found again at all, which may depend on the skill of whoever marks them, and whether
      a person's knots return to fixed places (as the perforator and nerve views expect) or arise anywhere and drift.
      Much else may rest on the answer.`,
    needs: `examiners, or people who can isolate their own knots · a few people, each seen several times · a Doppler or
      thermal map`,
  },
  {
    stage: 'with-knots-found',
    prong: 'knots',
    title: 'Whether knots grow with age',
    design: `Epidemiology: cross-sectional across ages, then a cohort followed over years`,
    what: `Knots might be counted and sized in people of all ages, from childhood to old age, and in the same people over
      years: by their own marks and by examiners (study 1), and by whichever instrument proves best (study 2). People
      might also be asked when their knots first appeared and how they have changed.`,
    show: `Whether knots grow larger and more numerous with age, and whether children are nearly free of them. The accounts
      part here: perforators, densification and nerves expect knots to rise with age (the first, in number and size);
      latches accumulate from early childhood; trigger points follow overload and injury; the motor switch remembers
      nothing by itself; perception follows history more than age.`,
    needs: `examiners or an instrument (studies 1 and 2) · people of all ages · a statistician`,
  },
  {
    stage: 'with-knots-found',
    prong: 'knots',
    title: 'What remains after death',
    design: `Post-mortem histology: knot sites against matched control sites`,
    what: `People whose knots were mapped in life (study 1), and who give their bodies to science, might be examined after
