---
<Doc
  title="Knots: an open problem, and ways to find out"
  kicker="For researchers"
  current="research"
  lede="People who work with the body find places that hold for years and let go in a breath, with a release the person feels at once. The aim is to find out exactly what this kind of knot is. Here are ideas for studies that might help, and a second question, what runaway release is, set out for those who know the methods to judge."
  description="An open problem in physiology: what a knot is, and what runaway release is. Fifteen ideas for studies that might help, which come first, how to respond, and the position paper."
  glyph="究"
  index={index}
  wide
>
  <section id="questions" class="sec full" data-glyph="問">
    <h2>Two open questions</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      <strong>What a knot is.</strong> Bodyworkers, people who foam-roll and contemplative practitioners all describe knots:
      discrete places in the body that hold, sometimes for years, and let go within seconds, under a hand, with the breath or
      to attention. A knot is felt as a bump, from very small to about the size of a penny; it seldom hurts unless pressed
      hard, as under a foam roller, so tenderness does not define it. Its release does. A knot lets go within seconds,
      most familiarly under a roller or a bodyworker's hand, and the person feels it go. A rush of feeling is often
      reported to follow at once: relief or well-being, at times tears or euphoria, and most of all when a whole cluster
      lets go together. These are the knots meant here; the feeling is given as a mark to know them by, not as something
      to seek. The aim is to find out exactly what this particular kind of knot is.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      A knot does not always stand out. An area with many knots seems to be less flexible, and is felt more as a dull
      block. A trained breath may be needed for the person to feel its knots as distinct bumps, and to release them;
      another's hand might then feel them too. As the knots let go, the block seems to ease. So an examiner's hand on a
      body at rest might find no bumps where there are many knots. The breath that releases knots is described as a
      trained one: a subtle weave of small in- and
      out-breaths, perhaps beginning with a small in-breath, builds the release, which is probably carried out on an
      out-breath that can be fairly strong, as an athlete's is. Under the pain of a foam roller the out-breaths are often
      strong and many, as in the breathing taught for childbirth.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      Knots are among the commonest things people report in their bodies and among the least explained, and nothing
      seems yet to have been recorded at the moment one lets go. Seven explanations have been proposed, in the vessels, the
      muscle, the fascia, the nerves or perception; each is set out at its strongest on the{' '}
      <a href={`${base}/hypotheses/`}>Hypotheses</a> page. Two are vascular: vasocomputation's latch, which proposes that
      held predictions are held vascular tension (<Fragment set:html={cite('johnson2023')} />), and the perforator view
      argued in the position paper below, which grew from it and from two reports of runaway release.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      <strong>What runaway release is.</strong> Two reports describe a rarer process that can follow forceful, long work at
      clearing knots. Releases come in chains that cannot be set down. The process does not come and go but runs on, over months
      or years, through phases: knots releasing, and the fascia felt to peel, hollow and fill in again over days to weeks (<a href={`${base}/research/perforator-hypothesis/#fascia`}
        >the position paper, Section 5</a>). Where many knots let go together, the skin over them can grow wet, then crust
      and flake away; unlike the rest, that can be seen and photographed. No study of it as such is known.
    </p>
    <p class="caution">
      Runaway release can be prolonged and dangerous (<a href={`${base}/about/#moderation`}>Moderation</a>; the position paper,
      Section 9). Anyone struggling can find support from <a href="https://www.cheetahhouse.org">Cheetah House</a>; anyone
      thinking about suicide should reach out now: in the US, call or text 988; elsewhere, find a line at{' '}
      <a href="https://findahelpline.com">findahelpline.com</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="help" class="sec full" data-glyph="助">
    <h2>What would help most</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The studies below are ideas from a non-expert working with AI, meant to show the kind of thing that might help.
      They were assessed only quickly, and some may prove not useful, or not worth doing now. Those who know the methods
      are better placed to judge: please make your own assessment of what would really be useful. Three judgements would
      help more than any one idea here:
    </p>
    <ul class="ask">
      <li><strong>The lowest-hanging fruit.</strong> Which could be done soon and cheaply, and still tell something?</li>
      <li><strong>What to address first.</strong> The list starts with finding knots, since much of the rest builds on
        it. Is that where to begin, and what should be taken up now?</li>
      <li><strong>What you could do.</strong> Which could you take on, with the skills, instruments, laboratory, patients or
        data you have, alone or with others?</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sub">
      Or propose something better: a study that would decide a question matters more than any listed here. A reading
      of the list helps even without an offer to take any of it on.
    </p>

    <h3 class="ask-h" id="respond">How to respond: a letter of intent</h3>
    <p class="sub">
      This work depends on your expertise, and a short letter is enough to give it: a page or less, which should not
      take long to write. For now, letters are asked for on the first question, what a knot is; the two studies on
      runaway release wait on fuller accounts of it.
    </p>
    <ul class="ask">
      <li><strong>Your reading of the list.</strong> Which studies are the lowest-hanging fruit, and which should be
        addressed first, including ones you would not take on yourself.</li>
      <li><strong>What you would do.</strong> The question, what you would measure, in whom and with what, and what the
        result might show. If it studies knots, say how you would know they are the ones meant here.</li>
      <li><strong>Who would do it.</strong> The people and their relevant experience, the instruments, laboratory,
        participants or data already to hand, and any competing interests.</li>
      <li><strong>Time and cost.</strong> A rough timeline and a rough total cost. If a smaller first step would still
        tell something, say what it is and what it would cost.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sub">
      One study, or part of one, is enough. Letters are public: post yours as an{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/ejhong/knots/issues">issue</a> on the project's repository, where others can read it
      and build on it. Objections are welcome there too. There is no deadline; letters are read as they arrive.
    </p>

    <h3 class="ask-h">How letters are weighed</h3>
    <ul class="ask">
      <li><strong>Usefulness now.</strong> What the study would settle, and how much of the rest depends on it.</li>
      <li><strong>Cost-effectiveness.</strong> What it would settle for what it would cost. Small, decisive studies are
        as welcome as large ones, and a low price alone is not enough.</li>
      <li><strong>Experience and capability.</strong> Whether the people, the instruments and the access to participants
        are in place to do it well and without delay.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="ask-h">What happens next</h3>
    <p class="sub">
      Every letter will be read, and promising ones followed up with questions. A small study may be funded on its
      letter and a conversation. Where a full proposal is needed, it will be asked for, and the time it takes to write
      will be paid for, whether or not the study is then funded.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      It is hoped that funded work will register its plan in public before data are collected, report whatever the
      result, share its data and code, and keep a simple public log of updates: frequent one-liners are ideal (“first
      three participants recorded”, “switched from thermal imaging to laser speckle”). These weigh in selection.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="studies" class="sec full" data-glyph="試">
    <span class="alias" id="experiments" />
    <h2>Studies that might help</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Fifteen ideas, grouped by what each needs and listed in the order they build on one another, not by importance. They
      are possibilities, assessed only quickly, and some may not be worth doing: your own assessment counts for more. Some
      are laboratory experiments; others are surveys, interviews, or new readings of tissue and records. Under each title is
      the kind of study it would be.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      Throughout, a knot means the kind described above, known by a release its owner feels. A study that marks or counts
      knots should say how it would know that they are of this kind; a bump or a tender spot is not enough.
    </p>
    {STAGES.map((st) => (
      <div class="stage" id={st.id}>
        <h3 class="stage-h">{st.title}</h3>
        <p class="sub" set:html={st.intro} />
        <div class="exps">
          {STUDIES.map((x, i) => x.stage === st.id && (
            <div class="exp" id={`study-${i + 1}`}>
              <span class="alias" id={`experiment-${i + 1}`} />
              {ALIAS[x.title] && <span class="alias" id={ALIAS[x.title]} />}
              <span class="dk mono">{i + 1} · {x.prong}</span>
              <p class="dt">{x.title}</p>
              <p class="design">{x.design}</p>
              <p set:html={x.what} />
              <p><strong>What it might show.</strong> <Fragment set:html={x.show} /></p>
              <p class="need mono">Likely needs: <Fragment set:html={x.needs} /></p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
    <p class="sub">
      Or one not listed here: anything that might help tell what a knot is, or what runaway release is.
    </p>
    <p class="caption">
      Any study with people belongs under ethics review and the site's moderation guidance (see{' '}
      <a href={`${base}/about/#moderation`}>About</a>), with comfortable pressure and a plan for referral. Breathing
      should be ordinary or, for people who already use a breath to release their knots, that breath as they already use
      it; no one should be taught a breath, or asked to press, breathe or work harder or longer, or to release more knots,
      than they usually do. None of this is a practice or a protocol.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="further" class="sec full" data-glyph="繋">
    <h2>The position paper</h2>
    <div class="docs">
      <a class="doc-card wide" href={`${base}/research/perforator-hypothesis/`}>
        <span class="dk mono">position paper · Zenodo preprint</span>
        <span class="dt">The perforator hypothesis: knots as small arteries held shut where they pierce the fascia</span>
        <span class="dd">The case for one mechanism, from established physiology: what it explains and what it does not, the
          alternatives in their strongest forms, the fascia, the acupuncture points, the dangers, and what would refute it.</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="sub">
      The paper is a preprint, written by AI under the author's direction and not peer-reviewed, and the author will not take
      it to a journal. If, having weighed the alternatives, you think the perforator hypothesis is the best fit, take it up:
      test it, rework it, publish it (the paper is CC BY 4.0). If you think a rival theory fits better, the models, data and
      code are open to you too.
    </p>
    <p class="links mono"><a href={`${base}/research/perforator-hypothesis.pdf`}>position paper (PDF)</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23901648">position paper (DOI)</a></p>
    <p class="contact">To take part, or to object: <a href="https://github.com/ejhong/knots/issues">issues</a> on the project's
      repository.</p>
  </section>
